"CSS Display Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-display-3/)\nciteturn1316view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-display-3/#box-generation\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1577\nL0: # CSS Display Module Level 3\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Editor’s Draft†www.w3.org , 5 June 2026\nL3: \nL4: More details about this document\nL5: This version:\nL6:     cite1†https://drafts.csswg.org/css-display/ L7: Latest published version:\nL8:     cite2†https://www.w3.org/TR/css-display-3/†www.w3.org L9: Previous Versions:\nL10:     cite3†https://www.w3.org/TR/2023/CR-css-display-3-20230330/†www.w3.org L11:     cite4†https://www.w3.org/TR/2021/CRD-css-display-3-20210903/†www.w3.org L12:     cite5†https://www.w3.org/TR/2020/CRD-css-display-3-20201218/†www.w3.org L13:     cite6†https://www.w3.org/TR/2020/CR-css-display-3-20200519/†www.w3.org L14:     cite7†https://www.w3.org/TR/2019/CR-css-display-3-20190711/†www.w3.org L15:     cite8†https://www.w3.org/TR/2018/CR-css-display-3-20180828/†www.w3.org L16:     cite9†https://www.w3.org/TR/2018/WD-css-display-3-20180809/†www.w3.org L17:     cite10†https://www.w3.org/TR/2018/WD-css-display-3-20180420/†www.w3.org L18:     cite11†https://www.w3.org/TR/2017/WD-css-display-3-20170720/†www.w3.org L19:     cite12†https://www.w3.org/TR/2017/WD-css-display-3-20170126/†www.w3.org L20:     cite13†https://www.w3.org/TR/2015/WD-css-display-3-20151015/†www.w3.org L21:     cite14†https://www.w3.org/TR/2015/WD-css-display-3-20150721/†www.w3.org L22:     cite15†https://www.w3.org/TR/2014/WD-css-display-3-20140911/†www.w3.org L23: Implementation Report:\nL24:     cite16†https://wpt.fyi/results/css/css-display?label=master&label=experimental&aligned†wpt.fyi L25: Feedback:\nL26:     cite17†CSSWG Issues Repository†github.com L27: Editors:\nL28:     cite18†Tab Atkins Jr.†xanthir.com (Google)\nL29:     cite19†Elika J. Etemad / fantasai†fantasai.inkedblade.net (Apple)\nL30: Suggest an Edit for this Spec:\nL31:     cite20†GitHub Editor†github.com L32: Test Suite:\nL33:     cite21†https://wpt.fyi/results/css/css-display/†wpt.fyi L34: cite22†Copyright†www.w3.org © 2026 cite23†World Wide Web Consortium†www.w3.org . W3C (World Wide Web Consortium)^{®} cite24†liability†www.w3.org , cite25†trademark†www.w3.org and cite26†permissive document license†www.w3.org rules apply.\nL35: \nL36: * * *\nL37: ## Abstract\nL38: \nL39: This module describes how the CSS formatting box tree is generated from the document element tree and defines the cite27†display property that controls it.\nL40: \nL41: cite28†CSS†www.w3.org is a language for describing the rendering of structured documents (such as HTML and XML) on screen, on paper, etc.\nL42: ## Status of this document\nL43: \nL44: This is a public copy of the editors’ draft. It is provided for discussion only and may change at any moment. Its publication here does not imply endorsement of its contents by W3C. Don’t cite this document other than as work in progress.\nL45: Please send feedback by cite29†filing issues in GitHub†github.com (preferred), including the spec code “css-display” in the title, like this: “[css-display] …summary of comment…”. All issues and comments are cite30†archived†lists.w3.org . Alternately, feedback can be sent to the (cite31†archived†lists.w3.org ) public mailing list www-style@w3.org.\nL46: \nL47: This document is governed by the cite32†18 August 2025 W3C Process Document†www.w3.org .\nL48: A cite33†preliminary implementation report†test.csswg.org is available. Further tests will be added during the CR period.\nL49: \nL50: The following features are at-risk, and may be dropped during the CR period:\nL51: \nL52:   * Application of cite34†::first-letter in the presence of run-ins\nL53:   * cite27†display: run-in L54:   * All multi-keyword values of cite27†display L55: “At-risk” is a W3C Process term-of-art, and does not necessarily imply that the feature is in danger of being dropped or delayed. It means that the WG believes the feature may have difficulty being interoperably implemented in a timely manner, and marking it as such allows the WG to drop the feature if necessary when transitioning to the Proposed Rec stage, without having to publish a new Candidate Rec without the feature first.\nL56: ## Table of Contents\nL57:   1. cite35†1 Introduction L58:     1. cite36†1.1 Module interactions L59:     2. cite37†1.2 Value Definitions L60:   2. cite38†2 Box Layout Modes: the display property L61:     1. cite39†2.1 Outer Display Roles for Flow Layout: the block, inline, and run-in keywords L62:     2. cite40†2.2 Inner Display Layout Models: the flow, flow-root, table, flex, grid, and ruby keywords L63:     3. cite41†2.3 Generating Marker Boxes: the list-item keyword L64:     4. cite42†2.4 Layout-Internal Display Types: the table-* and ruby-* keywords L65:     5. cite43†2.5 Box Generation: the none and contents keywords L66:     6. cite44†2.6 Precomposed Inline-level Display Values L67:     7. cite45†2.7 Automatic Box Type Transformations L68:     8. cite46†2.8 The Root Element’s Principal Box L69:   3. cite47†3 Display Order: the order property L70:     1. cite48†3.1 Reordering and Accessibility L71:   4. cite49†4 Invisibility: the visibility property L72:   5. cite50†5 Run-In Layout L73:   6. cite51†Appendix A: Glossary L74:   7. cite52†Appendix B: Effects of display: contents on Unusual Elements L75:     1. cite53†HTML Elements L76:     2. cite54†SVG Elements L77:     3. cite55†MathML Elements L78:   8. cite56†Appendix C: Box Construction Guidelines for Spec Authors L79:   9. cite57†Acknowledgments L80:   10. cite58†Changes L81:     1. cite59†Changes Since 2023 Candidate Recommendation Snapshot L82:     2. cite60†Changes Since 2020 Candidate Recommendation L83:     3. cite61†Changes Since 2019 Candidate Recommendation L84:     4. cite62†Changes Prior to Candidate Recommendation Status L85:   11. cite63†Privacy Considerations L86:     1. cite64†Security Considerations L87:     2. cite65†Self-Review Questionnaire L88:   12. cite66†Conformance L89:     1. cite67†Document conventions L90:     2. cite68†Conformance classes L91:     3. cite69†Partial implementations L92:       1. cite70†Implementations of Unstable and Proprietary Features L93:     4. cite71†Non-experimental implementations L94:   13. cite72†Index L95:     1. cite73†Terms defined by this specification L96:     2. cite74†Terms defined by reference L97:   14. cite75†References L98:     1. cite76†Normative References L99:     2. cite77†Non-Normative References L100:   15. cite78†Property Index L101: ## 1. Introduction\nL102: \nL103: This section is normative.\nL104: CSS takes a source document organized as a tree of elements (which can contain a mix of other cite79†elements and cite80†text nodes ) and text nodes (which can contain text), and renders it onto a cite81†canvas†www.w3.org such as your screen, a piece of paper, or an audio stream. Although any such source document can be rendered with CSS, the most commonly used type is the DOM. cite82†[DOM] (Some of these more complex tree types might have additional types of nodes, such as the comment nodes in the DOM.\nL105: For the purposes of CSS, all of these additional types of nodes are ignored, as if they didn’t exist.)\nL106: To do this, it generates an intermediary structure, the box tree, which represents the formatting structure of the rendered document. Each box in the cite83†box tree represents its corresponding cite79†element (or cite84†pseudo-element ) in space and/or time on the canvas, while each text sequence in the box tree likewise represents the corresponding contents of its cite80†text nodes .\nL107: To create the cite83†box tree , CSS first uses cite85†cascading and inheritance†www.w3.org , to assign a cite86†computed value for each CSS property to each cite79†element and cite80†text node in the source tree. (See cite87†[CSS-CASCADE-3] .)\nL108: Then, for each cite79†element , CSS generates zero or more cite88†boxes as specified by that element’s cite27†display property. Typically, an element generates a single box, the cite89†principal box , which represents itself and contains its contents in the cite83†box tree . However, some display values (e.g. display: list-item) generate more than one box (e.g. a cite90†principal block box†www.w3.org and a child cite91†marker box†www.w3.org ).\nL109: And some values (such as cite92†none or cite93†contents ) cause the element and/or its descendants to not generate any boxes at all. Boxes are often referred to by their display type—​e.g. a box generated by an element with display: block is called a “block box” or just a “block”.\nL110: A cite88†box is assigned the same styles as its generating cite79†element , unless otherwise indicated. In general, cite94†inherited properties are assigned to the cite89†principal box , and then inherit through the box tree to any other boxes generated by the same element.\nL111: Non-inherited properties default to applying to the principal box, but when the element generates multiple boxes, are sometimes defined to apply to a different box: for example, the cite95†border properties applied to a table element are applied to its cite96†table grid box , not to its principal cite97†table wrapper box .\nL112: If the value computation process alters the styles of those boxes, and the element’s style is requested (such as through `cite98†getComputedStyle() `), the element reflects, for each property, the value from the box to which that property was applied.\nL113: Similarly, each contiguous sequence of sibling cite80†text nodes generates a cite99†text sequence containing their text contents, which is assigned the same styles as the generating text nodes. If the sequence contains no text, however, it does not generate a text sequence.\nL114: In constructing the box tree, boxes generated by an element are descendants of the principal box of any ancestor elements. In the general case, the direct parent box of an element’s cite89†principal box is the principal box of its nearest ancestor element that generates a cite88†box ; however, there are some exceptions, such as for cite100†run-in boxes, display types (like tables) that generate multiple container boxes, and intervening cite101†anonymous boxes .\nL115: An anonymous box is a box that is not associated with any element. cite101†Anonymous boxes are generated in certain circumstances to fix up the cite83†box tree when it requires a particular nested structure that is not provided by the boxes generated from the cite102†element tree .\nL116: For example, a cite103†table cell box†www.w3.org requires a particular type of parent box (the cite103†table row box†www.w3.org ), and will generate an anonymous cite103†table row box†www.w3.org around itself if its parent is not a cite103†table row box†www.w3.org . (See cite104†[CSS2] § cite105†17.2.1†www.w3.org .) Unlike element-generated boxes, whose styles inherit strictly through the element tree, anonymous boxes (which only exist in the box tree) cite106†inherit†www.w3.org through their box tree parentage.\nL117: In the course of layout, cite88†boxes and cite99†text sequences can be broken into multiple cite107†fragments . This happens, for example, when an cite108†inline box and/or text sequence is broken across lines, or when a cite109†block box is broken across pages or columns, in a process called cite110†fragmentation . It can also happen due to bidi reordering of text (see cite111†Applying the Bidirectional Reordering Algorithm in cite112†CSS Writing Modes ) or higher-level cite113†display type box splitting, e.g.\nL118: cite114†block-in-inline splitting†www.w3.org (see cite115†CSS2§9.2†www.w3.org ) or cite116†column-spanner-in-block†www.w3.org splitting (see cite116†CSS Multi-column Layout†www.w3.org ). A box therefore consists of one or more cite117†box fragments , and a text sequence consists of one or more text fragments. See cite118†[CSS-BREAK-3] for more information on fragmentation.\nL119: Note: Many of the CSS specs were written before this terminology was ironed out, or refer to things incorrectly, so view older specs with caution when they’re using these terms. It should be possible to infer from context which term they really mean. Please cite119†report errors in specs when you find them, so they can be corrected.\nL120: Note: Further information on the “aural” box tree and its interaction with the cite27†display property can be found in the cite120†CSS Speech Module†www.w3.org . cite121†[CSS-SPEECH-1] L121: \nL122: Tests\nL123: \nL124:   * cite122†empty-text-baseline-001.html†wpt.fyi cite123†(live test)†wpt.live cite124†(source)†github.com L125:   * cite125†empty-text-baseline-002.html†wpt.fyi cite126†(live test)†wpt.live cite127†(source)†github.com L126: ### 1.1. Module interactions\nL127: \nL128: This module replaces and extends the definition of the cite27†display property defined in cite104†[CSS2] section 9.2.4.\nL129: \nL130: None of the properties in this module apply to the `::first-line` or `::first-letter` pseudo-elements.\nL131: \nL132: Tests\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Text Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text-3/)\nciteturn1316view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text-3/#line-breaking\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4797\n-------------------------------------------------